monitor: MON or WOZ [line]  then:  addr  addr.addr
         addr:b b b  addrR  X leaves
files:   DIR [A] [dir|pattern]
         (DIR APPS lists it; DIR *.PAS, DIR ?.TXT
         match here; * any run, ? any one)
         CD [dir]
         MKDIR dir
         RM name       RM -f name
         (RM moves it to /.TRASH -- it is not
         destroyed; RM -f is the one that really
         removes it.  DELETE -l lists the trash,
         DELETE -r name puts one back, DELETE -e
         empties it.  RANGER's DD uses the same
         trash, and so does DELETE name)
         RMDIR dir
         TYPE name
         EDIT [name]   VI [name]
         (full-screen editor: arrows Home End PgUp
         PgDn, Ctrl-O or Ctrl-S saves, Ctrl-X leaves.
         *SWAP EDIT name edits from inside a BASIC)
         (VI is modal and keeps the file in far
         memory: 32000 lines, 255 columns.
         /SYSTEM/ETC/VI.RC runs at startup, one ex
         command a line -- copy VI.SAMPLE there.
         hjkl 0 $ gg G, i a I A o O, x dd,
         :w :q :q! :wq, Esc leaves insert)
         XD name
         LOAD name [addr]
         SAVE name from.to
         RENAME old new
         CP old new
         RUN [name.prg|addr]
         EXEC script  (a # line is a comment)
         SWAP command
         (runs it on a clean machine and gives this
         one back: *SWAP name from a BASIC leaves
         the program in memory untouched)
         (a bare name is looked for in /SYSTEM/BIN,
         /APPS/NAME, /LANG/NAME, /HOME/PROJECTS/NAME
         and by extension in the language dirs)
         an unknown word runs name.prg from disk;
         F7 > Shell can also let it run name.COM
         on CP/M A: (off by default), with
         args (ARGS call $FF95)
         names starting with . are hidden from DIR
         (DIR A shows them)
memory:  FILL from.to value
         COPY from.to dest
system:  INFO
         TIME
         MODE [0-2] [0|1]
         (0 640x480, 1 640x240, 2 320x240)
         COLOR fg [bg]
         ECHO
         CLS
         CLG (clear the bitmap; *CLG in a BASIC)
         ALIAS [name [text]]
         (ALIAS lists; ALIAS LL DIR A defines;
         ALIAS LL with nothing after it removes.
         Kept in RAM: define them in STARTUP.BAT)
         HUSH
         RESET
         CAPSLOCK [ON|OFF]
         (toggle; letters typed come up uppercase --
         *CAPSLOCK in a BASIC)
         DUMP [note|ON|OFF]
         LOGO (clear the screen, redraw the banner)
         BENCH (25 s: measures the machine, sweeps
         the CPU clocks with a note on SID 0, and
         writes /SYSTEM/LOG/BENCH-NN.TXT -- a clock is
         right when it holds 60 fps with 0 gaps)
         BUG (asks you about a fault and writes the
         report to /SYSTEM/LOG/BUGREPORTS -- INFO -v
         names the build it happened on)
tongues: BBC (BBC BASIC on the Tube)
         CPM [command] (CP/M 2.2 on the Z80)
         !command / ! (the host's shell, K4510x only)
         (K: is the machine's own filesystem, so
         CP/M and K/OS can pass files both ways;
         P: is Turbo Pascal, D: is WordStar)
         (the command runs at boot; a name with no
         .COM runs its .SUB, so several lines --
         end with EXIT to leave CP/M as well)
         FORTH (Tali Forth 2, native)
         EHBASIC
         Mad Pascal: /LANG/PASCAL, C: /LANG/C, the
         sources beside their .prg
         PAS name / CC name (K4510x: compile
         name.PAS or name.C here into name.prg,
         then name runs it; tools/k4510-pas and
         k4510-cc on the Linux side)
network: a URL is a file name (Meatloaf):
         TYPE http://host/file
         CP http://host/x.prg x.prg
         RUN http://host/x.prg
         LOAD "http://..." in EhBASIC and BBC BASIC
         CD tnfs://host/dir puts the current directory
         on a TNFS server (FujiNet, Meatloaf):
         DIR lists it, bare names load from it,
         CD .. climbs,
         CD - comes home
         TELNET host port (F12 hangs up)
         on the N: device at $D900; ANSI/VT100 via JIM
terminal: JIM at $DA00, a VT100/ANSI in hardware:
         CP/M programs and BBC BASIC draw through it
         (install CP/M programs for VT100 or ANSI);
         TELNET too
host:    F7 opens the settings menu (border, font,
         resolution, scanlines, scaling, volume,
         keys, reset, Run STARTUP.BAT);
         Super+PgUp resets
boot:    /STARTUP.BAT runs as an EXEC script at
         power-on.  If it wedges the machine,
         turn it off in F7 > Run STARTUP.BAT, or
         start with --no-startup.bat
